%[text] %[text:anchor:H_85A1BCF1] ## Introduction
%[text] The Fourier transform can be used to solve for the frequency content of an analytic function.
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_6D7E5044] ![sineGausRealImaginary](text:image:16fb)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Gaussian-modulated sinusoid in the time and frequency domains.*
%[text] However, real signals are typically discrete sequences of measured values, not analytic functions. For such signals, the Fourier transform cannot be applied directly.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![sineGaussSampleToWhat](text:image:1905)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *A discrete signal* $\\{ y\_n \\}${"altText":"\{ y\_n \}"} *sampled from an analytic function. What will it look like in the frequency domain?*
%[text] To analyze a discrete signal in the frequency domain, you can use the *Discrete Fourier Transform* (DFT). The DFT is probably the most widely used Fourier analysis tool since most real signals contain discrete measurements. You'll often find the DFT under a different name: the FFT (*Fast Fourier Transform*). Don't be confused, though; they're fundamentally the same. The FFT is simply an algorithm that computes the DFT, you guessed it, faster.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7871CDD4] ## Definition
%[text] The DFT is similar to the complex Fourier series and Fourier Transform. The essential difference is that it operates entirely in terms of discrete sequences, without any reference to continuous functions. The DFT is defined as
%[text]{"align":"center"} $Y\_k = \\sum\_{n = 0}^{N-1} y\_n e^{- \\frac{ 2 \\pi i k n }{N }${"altText":"Y\_k = \sum\_{n = 0}^{N-1} y\_n e^{- \frac{ 2 \pi i k n }{N }"}
%[text] The result is a sequence of $N${"altText":"N"}discrete values $\\{ Y\_k \\}${"altText":"\{ Y\_k \}"} that represent $\\{ y\_n \\}${"altText":"\{ y\_n \}"} in the frequency domain. The result $\\{ Y\_k \\}${"altText":"\{ Y\_k \}"} is analogous to the Fourier transform $\\hat{y}(\\omega)${"altText":"\hat{y}(\omega)"}, but more on that later.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1AA1D78F] ## Computing the DFT
%[text] ![write](text:image:7ed1) **Example.** In this example, the DFT of a synthesized audio signal is computed directly from the definition.
%[text] **Task.** First, create the data points by running this section.
%[text] ![matlab](text:image:00a0) **Pro-tip.** To run a section quickly, use the keyboard shortcut, **Ctrl**+**Enter**.
[t,y,Fs] = sineGaussData; % Helper function that generates the data

figure
plot(t,y,'.')
xlabel("t (s)")
ylabel("y")
%[text] Try zooming in on the signal (use the zoom tool ![](text:image:321b)) to examine the discrete data points $y\_n${"altText":"y\_n"} more closely. Do you see a pattern?
%%
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to play the generated signal as an audio clip.
% This code will play the audio signal
soundsc(y,Fs)
%%
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to compute the DFT using the definition. Note that the [`tic`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/tic.html) and [`toc`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/toc.html) functions are used to measure the time required to compute the N-point DFT.
N = numel(y); % The number of samples in y
n = 0:(N-1); % Indices
Y = zeros(N,1);
tic
% Loop through the indices of Yk
for k = 0:(N-1)
    % Compute the sum and put the result in Y
    Y(k+1) = sum( y.*exp(-2*pi*1i*k*n/N) );
end
toc
%%
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to plot the real and imaginary components of the DFT stored in `Y`.
plot(real(Y),".-")
hold on
plot(imag(Y),".-")
hold off
xlabel("k")
ylabel("Y_k")
% xlim([300 350])
%[text] - Try zooming in on the signal to view the DFT near the peaks. You can use the zoom tool (![](text:image:9bb2)) or uncomment the line `xlim([300 350])`. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_F52E2FE8] ### Fast Fourier Transform (FFT)
%[text] In the previous example, the DFT was computed using a for loop, requiring $O(N^2)${"altText":"O(N^2)"} computations. The notation $O(N^2)${"altText":"O(N^2)"} means that the computational cost scales as the square of $N${"altText":"N"} (the number of data points). This is very inefficient relative to the Fast Fourier Transform (FFT). The FFT algorithm breaks up the DFT into smaller DFTs, and then combines the results to form the full DFT. The FFT requires only $O(N \\log(N))${"altText":"O(N \log(N))"} operations. This implies the FFT can be thousands of times faster than the DFT for large data sets.
%[text] ![write](text:image:6730) **Exercise.** In this exercise, you will compute the DFT using the FFT algorithm.
%[text] **Task 1.** First, create the data points by running this section.
[t,y,Fs] = sineGaussData; % Helper function that generates the data
%%
%[text] **Task 2.** Use the [`fft`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/fft.html) function to compute the DFT of `y` and store the result in Y.
% Write your code here


%%
%[text] **Task 3.** Surround the `fft` function call in **Task 2** with the [`tic`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/tic.html) and [`toc`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/toc.html) commands to measure how long it takes to compute the FFT. Compare this to the amount of time required using the definition in the previous section. How much faster is the FFT?
%%
%[text] **Task 4.** Plot the real and imaginary components of `Y`. To extract the real and imaginary components, use the [`real`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/real.html) and [`imag`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/imag.html) functions. Use [`hold`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/hold.html) commands to continue plotting on the same axes.
% Write your code here


%[text] If correct, your result should be identical to the DFT plot from the previous example.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E0BB75DF] ## Comparing the DFT to the Fourier Transform
%[text] The Gaussian-modulated sinusoid is useful for comparing the DFT and Fourier transform because the analytic form is known. You probably noticed that the DFT looks similar to what you'd expect from the Fourier transform of the Gaussian-modulated sinusoid. 
%[text] The discrete data points in the previous section were sampled from a continuous function. To better understand what is computed by the DFT, it's helpful to look at the Fourier transform of the original function.
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to show the analytic plot. 
plotFourierTransform
% xlim([650 750])
  %[control:button:909b]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] ![brightbulb](text:image:17e1) **Reflect.** 
%[text] - Which peaks in the DFT correspond to the peaks of the Fourier transform?
%[text] - Try zooming in on the signal to view the DFT near the peaks. You can use the zoom tool (![](text:image:0d5c)) or uncomment the line `xlim([650 750]).`
%[text] - How does the Fourier transform of the signal compare to the DFT? \
%%
%[text] To understand the differences between the Fourier transform and the DFT plots, compare the DFT and Fourier transform definitions. The DFT:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $Y\_k = \\sum\_{n = 0}^{N-1} y\_n e^{- \\frac{ 2 \\pi i k n }{N }${"altText":"Y\_k = \sum\_{n = 0}^{N-1} y\_n e^{- \frac{ 2 \pi i k n }{N }"}
%[text] The Fourier transform:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\hat{y}(\\omega) = \\int\_{-\\infty}^\\infty y(t) e^{-i\\omega t} \\ dt${"altText":"\hat{y}(\omega) = \int\_{-\infty}^\infty y(t) e^{-i\omega t} \ dt"}
%[text] Notice the similarities:
%[text] - The DFT sum approximates the integral
%[text] - The two factors correspond between definitions $ y\_n \\leftrightarrow y(t)${"altText":" y\_n \leftrightarrow y(t)"} and $e^{- \\frac{ 2 \\pi i k n }{N }} \\leftrightarrow  e^{-i\\omega t} ${"altText":"e^{- \frac{ 2 \pi i k n }{N }} \leftrightarrow  e^{-i\omega t} "}.  \
%[text] Notice two essential differences:
%[text] 1. The $dt${"altText":"dt"} of the integral scales the Fourier transform $\\hat{y}${"altText":"\hat{y}"}, but the DFT result, $Y\_k${"altText":"Y\_k"}, is not scaled by a $\\Delta t${"altText":"\Delta t"}.
%[text] 2. The complex exponential is given in terms of discrete indices: $k${"altText":"k"} and $n${"altText":"n"}, rather than continuous variables, $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"} and $t${"altText":"t"}. \
%[text] ![write](text:image:44aa) **Example.** In the example, the DFT will be related to the Fourier transform using the Gaussian-modulated sinusoid.
%[text] **Task**. Run this section to compute the DFT using the [`fft`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/fft.html) function.
[t,y,Fs] = sineGaussData; % Helper function that generates the data
Y = fft(y);
%%
%[text] The Fourier transform is scaled (in amplitude) by $dt${"altText":"dt"}. The discrete analog of $dt${"altText":"dt"} is the time step $\\Delta t${"altText":"\Delta t"} in the sampled signal. The time step $\\Delta t = 1/ f\_s${"altText":"\Delta t = 1\/ f\_s"}, where $f\_s${"altText":"f\_s"} is the sampling frequency. This implies that, to rescale the output $Y${"altText":"Y"} to match $\\hat{y}${"altText":"\hat{y}"}, you can multiply by `1/Fs`.
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to rescale `Y` and plot the result. Compare the vertical axis scale to the result of the analytic Fourier transform.
N = length(Y);
k = 0:(N-1);
Yrescaled = 1/Fs*Y;
realImagPlot(k,Yrescaled,"$k$","$\frac{1}{f_s}Y_k$"); % Helper function that creates the plot
%%
%[text] You can also use the sample rate $f\_s${"altText":"f\_s"} to relate the discrete indices $k${"altText":"k"} and $n${"altText":"n"} to the continuous variables $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"} and $t${"altText":"t"}. Equating these terms in the exponentials of each definition:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $  \\frac{2 \\pi kn}{N} = t \\omega${"altText":"  \frac{2 \pi kn}{N} = t \omega"}
%[text] Discretizing $t${"altText":"t"} as $n \\Delta t  = n / f\_s${"altText":"n \Delta t  = n \/ f\_s"} implies that the discrete frequencies are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $\\omega\_k = \\frac{ 2 \\pi k f\_s}{   N}${"altText":"\omega\_k = \frac{ 2 \pi k f\_s}{   N}"}
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to plot the DFT result against the angular frequencies $\\omega\_k${"altText":"\omega\_k"}.
omega = 2*pi*k*Fs/N;
realImagPlot(omega,Yrescaled,"$\omega$","$\frac{1}{f_s}Y_k$"); % Helper function that creates the plot
%%
%[text] Compare the angular frequencies of the DFT along the $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"}-axis to those of the Fourier transform. Are the peaks in the same location? You can identify the locations of the peaks by clicking on the graph to create a tooltip. 
%[text] You should notice that the **first** peak of the DFT matches the **second** peak of the Fourier transform. This results from the definition of the DFT (which has unexpected consequences). You can use the [`fftshift`](https://www.mathworks.com/help/matlab/ref/fftshift.html) function to get the DFT lined up with the Fourier transform.
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to rearrange the DFT values, $\\{ Y\_k \\}${"altText":"\{ Y\_k \}"}, so that they line up with the Fourier transform $\\hat{y}${"altText":"\hat{y}"} . The values of $\\omega${"altText":"\omega"} are also regenerated to correspond to the shifted frequencies.
% Shift the Y-values
Yshift = fftshift(Yrescaled);
% Shift the angular frequency
omegaShift = 2*pi*Fs/N*(-N/2:N/2-1);

realImagPlot(omegaShift,Yshift,"$\omega$","$\frac{1}{f_s}Y_k$"); % Helper function that creates the plot
xlim([-1000 1000])
%%
%[text] **Task.** Run this section to plot the shifted DFT values (`Yshift)` alongside the Fourier transform.
plotDFTandTransform(omegaShift,Yshift);
% xlim([650,750])
%[text] - Try zooming in on the signal to view the DFT near the peaks. You can use the zoom tool (![](text:image:5c17)) or uncomment the `xlim([650,750])` line. \
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_AFE0D2A6] ## Analyzing an Audio Signal
